<refentry id="cypher-next">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-next">
  <primary>NEXT</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>NEXT</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>NEXT</refname>
  <refpurpose>chain two queries end to end</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
<replaceable class="parameter">query</replaceable> NEXT <replaceable class="parameter">query</replaceable> [ NEXT <replaceable class="parameter">query</replaceable> ] ...
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-next-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>NEXT</command> turns the result table of the query on its left into the driving
   table of the query on its right. It does at the boundary between two whole queries what
   <command>WITH</command> does between clauses inside one query.
  </para>

  <para>
   The query to the left of <command>NEXT</command> has to end with
   <command>RETURN</command>. The boundary resets scope: only the columns that
   <command>RETURN</command> produced are visible on the right, and every other variable is
   gone. Columns cross the boundary in their own types, so a vertex is still a vertex, an edge
   is still an edge, and a path is still a path.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>NEXT</command> is left associative, so <literal>A NEXT B NEXT C</literal> composes
   as <literal>(A NEXT B) NEXT C</literal>. A <xref linkend="cypher-union"/>,
   <literal>INTERSECT</literal> or <literal>EXCEPT</literal> composite may stand on either
   side.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>NEXT</command> is accepted at the start of a statement and inside
   <command>EXPLAIN</command> and <command>PREPARE</command>. It is not accepted inside a
   subquery, in either a <literal>CALL { }</literal> body or a subquery value expression.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-next-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">query</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A complete query. The one to the left of <command>NEXT</command> has to end with
      <command>RETURN</command>, and every column it returns has to be named. An unaliased
      expression raises <literal>expression in WITH must be aliased (use AS)</literal>.
     </para>
     <para>
      The query to the right is a fresh one. It may begin with any clause that can open a
      query: <command>MATCH</command>, <command>OPTIONAL MATCH</command>,
      <command>CREATE</command>, <command>INSERT</command>, <command>MERGE</command>,
      <command>LOAD FROM</command>, <command>UNWIND</command>, <command>FOR</command>,
      <command>CALL</command>, <command>LET</command>, <command>RETURN</command> or
      <command>FINISH</command>. It may also begin with a clause that acts on the carried
      table: <command>WITH</command>, <command>FILTER</command>,
      <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command>, <literal>OFFSET</literal> or
      <command>LIMIT</command>. It ends the way any query ends, with
      <command>RETURN</command>, <command>FINISH</command> or a write clause.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-next-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Carry the films one director made into a second query that finds their casts:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN m
NEXT
MATCH (a:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m)
RETURN m.title AS film, a.name AS actor ORDER BY film;

      film      |         actor
----------------+-----------------------
 "Inception"    | "Leonardo DiCaprio"
 "Interstellar" | "Matthew McConaughey"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An intermediate <command>WITH</command> composes the same two steps inside one query and
   plans identically. <literal>m</literal> crosses the boundary as a vertex, so the second
   query traverses from it again.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>FILTER</command> cannot open a standalone query, but it can open the query after
   <command>NEXT</command>. That gives an aggregate the equivalent of SQL's
   <literal>HAVING</literal> without an extra <command>WITH</command>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN p.name AS actor, count(m) AS films
NEXT
FILTER films &gt; 2
RETURN actor, films ORDER BY actor;

          actor          | films
-------------------------+-------
 "Arnold Schwarzenegger" | 3
 "Keanu Reeves"          | 4
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Each <command>NEXT</command> is another boundary, evaluated left to right:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN m.genre AS genre
NEXT
RETURN DISTINCT genre AS g
NEXT
RETURN count(g) AS genres;

 genres
--------
 3
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Only returned columns survive the boundary, so a reference to anything else fails at parse
   time:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN m
NEXT
RETURN p.name;

ERROR:  variable does not exist
LINE 4: RETURN p.name;
               ^
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-next-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>NEXT</command> costs nothing at run time. The boundary is built by rewriting the
   left query's <command>RETURN</command> into the <command>WITH</command> that the clause
   pipeline already places between clause groups.
  </para>

  <para>
   Two guards report misuse directly rather than as a syntax error. A left query that does not
   end with <command>RETURN</command> raises
   <literal>a query statement before NEXT must end with RETURN</literal>. A left query ending
   in <command>FINISH</command> raises <literal>FINISH cannot be followed by NEXT</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The last query in a chain still has to end the statement properly.
   <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command>, <literal>OFFSET</literal> and
   <command>LIMIT</command> are clauses in their own right, but none of them can terminate a
   query. A chain that stops on one raises
   <literal>Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause</literal>. Follow it
   with <command>RETURN</command> or <command>FINISH</command>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>next</literal> is an unreserved keyword, so it stays available as a variable, a
   label or a property name.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-next-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>NEXT</command> implements the GQL next statement of the procedure body, ISO/IEC
   39075:2024 clause 9.2, feature GQ20. Chaining query statements, left associative
   multi chaining, the scope reset at the boundary and a set operation composite on either
   side all work as the standard describes. The optional <literal>YIELD</literal> clause that
   the standard allows between <command>NEXT</command> and the following statement is not
   accepted and raises a syntax error. Project and rename in the preceding
   <command>RETURN</command> instead.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher has no <command>NEXT</command>. Composition there is always the
   <command>WITH</command> form, which AgensGraph accepts unchanged.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

